WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.275
Welcome to Module 3 of the
Non-Person Identity learning path:

2
00:00:04.275 --> 00:00:06.388
decide each identity's target.

3
00:00:06.388 --> 00:00:10.338
You have a register from Module
1 and its owners from Module 2.

4
00:00:10.338 --> 00:00:14.526
Now every identity needs a target:
where it ends up, and when.

5
00:00:14.526 --> 00:00:19.474
This module is Gate 2, Decision, with
Chapter 3 of the developer workbook,

6
00:00:19.474 --> 00:00:23.500
which turns the program's
migration path matrix into code.

7
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:29.031
You'll run the matrix on the fixtures, read
the owners' decisions and the waves they

8
00:00:29.031 --> 00:00:34.150
fall in, scope the Workload I D Premium
license, and see why Gate 2 isn't met yet.

9
00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:38.088
The code runs on your laptop,
against the same fictional estate.

10
00:00:38.088 --> 00:00:43.450
It's illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not run against a live tenant.

11
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.037
Every identity ends in one of five places.

12
00:00:50.037 --> 00:00:51.038
Retire it.

13
00:00:51.038 --> 00:00:54.850
Move it to a group managed or
delegated managed service account.

14
00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:59.750
Or move it to a managed identity, a
federated credential, or a certificate.

15
00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:04.672
The program's migration path matrix,
which is design guidance, decides by the

16
00:01:04.672 --> 00:01:07.484
account type and by
where the workload runs,

17
00:01:07.484 --> 00:01:10.062
and its rule is simple: no stored secrets.

18
00:01:10.062 --> 00:01:14.288
But three things are easy to blur
here, and the code keeps them apart.

19
00:01:14.288 --> 00:01:16.300
The matrix proposes a target.

20
00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:20.425
The application owner decides the
disposition, account by account.

21
00:01:20.425 --> 00:01:22.975
And the ISSO checks the gate.

22
00:01:22.975 --> 00:01:25.628
Keep those three separate,
and nobody can say the

23
00:01:25.628 --> 00:01:28.413
code made a decision that
a person should have made.

24
00:01:28.413 --> 00:01:34.590
In this module you run all three on the
fixtures, and read why Gate 2 isn't met yet.

25
00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:41.180
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

26
00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:43.827
which takes about fifty-five
minutes with the lab.

27
00:01:43.827 --> 00:01:47.513
You'll explain the difference
between the matrix's proposal,

28
00:01:47.513 --> 00:01:49.577
the owner's disposition, and Gate 2.

29
00:01:49.577 --> 00:01:52.038
You'll name the target the matrix proposes

30
00:01:52.038 --> 00:01:54.639
for each account type
and workload location.

31
00:01:54.639 --> 00:01:59.040
You'll run the matrix on the fixtures
and explain each of its findings.

32
00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.860
And you'll read a wave's rollback,
and say which identities Conditional

33
00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:06.878
Access for workload identities can
cover now, and which after conversion.

34
00:02:06.878 --> 00:02:09.642
Before you start, finish Modules 1 and 2,

35
00:02:09.642 --> 00:02:12.752
because the matrix reads
both of their runs.

36
00:02:12.752 --> 00:02:17.902
And read Chapter 9 of Identity and
Directory, on the migration path matrix.

37
00:02:17.902 --> 00:02:22.939
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7.1 or
later with Pester 5, and a clone.

38
00:02:22.939 --> 00:02:24.210
No tenant.

39
00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.422
Unit 1 is the guide's Step 2,
and the slide shows part of its

40
00:02:31.422 --> 00:02:35.485
condensed decision table, which
is the program's design guidance.

41
00:02:35.485 --> 00:02:40.397
Anything disabled, unused or with
no owner after Step 1 is retired.

42
00:02:40.397 --> 00:02:43.390
A traditional account for
an Azure workload goes

43
00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:46.236
to a managed identity,
user-assigned preferred.

44
00:02:46.236 --> 00:02:49.990
One calling Entra-protected A
P I's from a host with an O I D

45
00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:54.235
C issuer gets an app registration
with a federated credential.

46
00:02:54.235 --> 00:02:59.406
A Kerberos-only account on premises goes
to a group managed service account,

47
00:02:59.406 --> 00:03:02.772
or a delegated one on a
Windows Server 2025 domain.

48
00:03:02.772 --> 00:03:07.297
A managed service account is upgraded
to a group managed one first, because,

49
00:03:07.297 --> 00:03:11.968
as Microsoft Learn's delegated managed
service account documentation says,

50
00:03:11.968 --> 00:03:14.522
neither can be migrated
to a delegated one.

51
00:03:14.522 --> 00:03:18.030
And a client secret is
replaced: a managed identity,

52
00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.535
then federation, then a certificate.

53
00:03:20.535 --> 00:03:22.285
Two more points from Step 2.

54
00:03:22.285 --> 00:03:24.047
High-risk accounts go first.

55
00:03:24.047 --> 00:03:29.078
And a group managed account needs a K
D S root key, created once per domain;

56
00:03:29.078 --> 00:03:32.914
Microsoft Learn's Create a
K D S root key documentation

57
00:03:32.914 --> 00:03:37.190
says domain controllers wait
up to ten hours after that.

58
00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:43.290
Unit 2 is the matrix as code.

59
00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:46.877
The proposal gives every
identity in scope a target.

60
00:03:46.877 --> 00:03:50.546
SQL Server accounts are handed
off, and an account found

61
00:03:50.546 --> 00:03:54.364
only by a password that never
expires waits for confirmation.

62
00:03:54.364 --> 00:03:58.158
For the rest, retire comes
first: for an account Module 2

63
00:03:58.158 --> 00:04:02.203
found unowned and nobody claimed,
or one already disabled.

64
00:04:02.203 --> 00:04:06.252
Otherwise the first matrix row
that matches proposes the target.

65
00:04:06.252 --> 00:04:11.075
Rows one to five send a traditional
account off premises to a managed identity,

66
00:04:11.075 --> 00:04:15.303
or an app registration with a
federated credential or a certificate.

67
00:04:15.303 --> 00:04:19.772
Rows six and seven send a Kerberos
account to a delegated managed service

68
00:04:19.772 --> 00:04:24.315
account where the domain and hosts allow
one, otherwise a group managed one.

69
00:04:24.315 --> 00:04:27.849
Rows eight to thirteen handle
the managed service accounts,

70
00:04:27.849 --> 00:04:32.727
fourteen to eighteen the app registrations,
and nineteen keeps a managed identity.

71
00:04:32.727 --> 00:04:37.300
The workload facts come from the owner,
and without them there's no proposal.

72
00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:44.370
The owner's disposition
is read row by row,

73
00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:49.837
and what's missing or wrong becomes a
finding, N P I M A T oh one to oh nine.

74
00:04:49.837 --> 00:04:51.826
Oh one: no disposition.

75
00:04:51.826 --> 00:04:55.825
Oh two: no proposal, because
the workload facts are missing.

76
00:04:55.825 --> 00:04:57.844
Oh three: a target that doesn't fit,

77
00:04:57.844 --> 00:05:02.779
such as a managed or group managed service
account sent to a delegated one, which

78
00:05:02.779 --> 00:05:05.621
Microsoft Learn's
delegated managed service

79
00:05:05.621 --> 00:05:08.163
account documentation says can't be done.

80
00:05:08.163 --> 00:05:12.058
Oh four: the owner chose a target
the matrix didn't propose;

81
00:05:12.058 --> 00:05:14.876
that's allowed, with a
reason on the record.

82
00:05:14.876 --> 00:05:18.989
Oh five: an account kept in Active
Directory with no end date,

83
00:05:18.989 --> 00:05:21.088
or one past the program's dates.

84
00:05:21.088 --> 00:05:25.412
Oh six: an account kept on
Kerberos with no S P N owner.

85
00:05:25.412 --> 00:05:29.475
Oh seven: a high-risk account
re-platformed outside wave 1.

86
00:05:29.475 --> 00:05:33.762
Oh eight: a wave with no rollback
owner, or no review date.

87
00:05:33.762 --> 00:05:37.480
And oh nine is the Workload
I D Premium scope.

88
00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:42.442
Three more pieces.

89
00:05:42.442 --> 00:05:43.817
First, the waves.

90
00:05:43.817 --> 00:05:46.959
Each wave's rollback is built
from its members' targets:

91
00:05:46.959 --> 00:05:51.216
re-enable the legacy account and revert
the application's configuration,

92
00:05:51.216 --> 00:05:53.799
and for a delegated
managed service account,

93
00:05:53.799 --> 00:05:58.335
the undo command from Microsoft Learn's
Setting up delegated Managed Service

94
00:05:58.335 --> 00:06:02.593
Accounts documentation, with the
superseded account kept, never deleted.

95
00:06:02.593 --> 00:06:08.043
Second, decision D N P 4, on the
Workload I D Premium license.

96
00:06:08.043 --> 00:06:12.593
Microsoft Learn's Conditional Access
for workload identities documentation

97
00:06:12.593 --> 00:06:17.143
says it covers only single-tenant service
principals registered in the tenant.

98
00:06:17.143 --> 00:06:20.405
Multitenant apps and managed
identities aren't covered.

99
00:06:20.405 --> 00:06:23.225
The program's position,
which is design guidance,

100
00:06:23.225 --> 00:06:27.314
is to license the single-tenant
service principals classified Tier 1,

101
00:06:27.314 --> 00:06:30.768
and the code counts who's eligible
now and after conversion.

102
00:06:30.768 --> 00:06:31.867
It buys nothing.

103
00:06:31.867 --> 00:06:37.635
Third, Gate 2 itself, the ISSO's: five
criteria, each reported with its open

104
00:06:37.635 --> 00:06:44.140
findings, and the gate is met only when
no finding of severity Finding is open.

105
00:06:47.890 --> 00:06:48.615
Try it.

106
00:06:48.615 --> 00:06:53.412
Step one evaluates Microsoft Learn's
two service account decision tables,

107
00:06:53.412 --> 00:06:58.441
from Securing on-premises service
accounts and Securing cloud-based service

108
00:06:58.441 --> 00:07:01.613
accounts, exactly as
published: twelve tests pass,

109
00:07:01.613 --> 00:07:04.553
and the thirty-one not
run are the example's own.

110
00:07:04.553 --> 00:07:08.788
Step two lists what the matrix
needs: two read permissions, License

111
00:07:08.788 --> 00:07:13.564
Assignment dot Read dot All and User dot
Read dot All, and only the GET method.

112
00:07:13.564 --> 00:07:18.253
Step three runs Modules 1 and
2 quietly, then the matrix.

113
00:07:18.253 --> 00:07:22.785
Twenty-one identities are in scope
and twenty get a proposal: six retire,

114
00:07:22.785 --> 00:07:27.471
seven keep, two group managed and two
delegated managed service accounts,

115
00:07:27.471 --> 00:07:30.928
two managed identities, and
one federated credential.

116
00:07:30.928 --> 00:07:33.981
The owners have decided
eighteen of twenty-two,

117
00:07:33.981 --> 00:07:37.427
in two waves, and each wave
holds one high-risk account.

118
00:07:37.427 --> 00:07:42.952
The license decision is Tier 1, two in
scope, and the tenant holds two units.

119
00:07:42.952 --> 00:07:46.502
Seven findings, four warnings,
one information item.

120
00:07:46.502 --> 00:07:48.860
And the last line: Gate 2 is not met,

121
00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:52.960
with the open findings for
each of its five criteria.

122
00:07:56.710 --> 00:07:58.723
Step four reads the proposals.

123
00:07:58.723 --> 00:08:03.209
Six are retire: Module 2's
orphans and the disabled accounts.

124
00:08:03.209 --> 00:08:07.121
svc hrsync and svc
webfront go to a delegated

125
00:08:07.121 --> 00:08:10.610
managed service account, matrix row six.

126
00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:15.117
Payroll Export and Deploy Bot go to
a managed identity, row fourteen,

127
00:08:15.117 --> 00:08:19.060
and Mail Automation to a
federated credential, row fifteen.

128
00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:23.485
HR Connector has no proposal, because
nobody recorded where it runs.

129
00:08:23.485 --> 00:08:25.998
Step five reads the twelve findings.

130
00:08:25.998 --> 00:08:34.022
svc hrsync is high risk, but scheduled in
wave 2, so that's N P I M A T oh seven.

131
00:08:34.022 --> 00:08:39.197
svc webfront is kept on Kerberos
with no S P N owner, oh six.

132
00:08:39.197 --> 00:08:45.798
The delegated account dmsa hrsync
has no end date before G 5, oh five.

133
00:08:45.798 --> 00:08:48.823
Wave 2 has no rollback owner, oh eight.

134
00:08:48.823 --> 00:08:54.370
And Vendor Portal is multitenant, so
Conditional Access can't cover it, oh nine.

135
00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:01.058
Step six prints each
wave with its rollback.

136
00:09:01.058 --> 00:09:06.123
Wave 1 is Mail Automation, with a rollback
owner, and its rollback re-enables the

137
00:09:06.123 --> 00:09:11.657
legacy credential, reverts the configuration
and removes the federated credential.

138
00:09:11.657 --> 00:09:19.083
Wave 2 holds svc hrsync, svc intranet, a
managed service account and Payroll Export.

139
00:09:19.083 --> 00:09:22.501
svc intranet is there because
its owner re-platforms it

140
00:09:22.501 --> 00:09:26.069
to the group managed service
account the matrix proposes.

141
00:09:26.069 --> 00:09:28.557
And the wave's rollback owner is empty.

142
00:09:28.557 --> 00:09:32.208
That's the N P I M A T oh eight finding.

143
00:09:32.208 --> 00:09:37.080
Notice the delegated managed account's
rollback is the undo command from Microsoft

144
00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:40.491
Learn's delegated managed
service account documentation,

145
00:09:40.491 --> 00:09:43.345
and the superseded account
is kept, never deleted.

146
00:09:43.345 --> 00:09:46.970
Step seven reads the
Workload I D Premium scope.

147
00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:50.181
Six single-tenant app
registrations can be covered by

148
00:09:50.181 --> 00:09:53.320
Conditional Access today,
and four after conversion.

149
00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:57.119
Payroll Export is one that
leaves: its owner's decision moves

150
00:09:57.119 --> 00:10:01.070
it to a managed identity, and
managed identities aren't covered.

151
00:10:01.070 --> 00:10:05.283
Step eight runs every test:
five hundred thirteen pass.

152
00:10:05.283 --> 00:10:09.000
Then delete the output, which
names accounts and owners.

153
00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:17.812
Lab 2 runs the matrix against your
own test tenant, worldwide or G C C,

154
00:10:17.812 --> 00:10:20.942
never production, with fictional people,

155
00:10:20.942 --> 00:10:24.900
and with the Lab 2 runs of
Modules 1 and 2 already done.

156
00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:26.638
The run only reads.

157
00:10:26.638 --> 00:10:29.851
Grant User dot Read dot
All and License Assignment

158
00:10:29.851 --> 00:10:32.375
dot Read dot All to
Module 1's reading app.

159
00:10:32.375 --> 00:10:35.246
Write the owners' inputs
for your test accounts:

160
00:10:35.246 --> 00:10:38.338
where each workload runs,
and each owner's decision.

161
00:10:38.338 --> 00:10:39.587
Run the matrix.

162
00:10:39.587 --> 00:10:41.619
Record what the license read shows,

163
00:10:41.619 --> 00:10:45.752
and check in the admin center which
principals Conditional Access can

164
00:10:45.752 --> 00:10:49.675
actually target, because that's the
scope the D N P 4 decision rests on.

165
00:10:49.675 --> 00:10:54.287
To back it out, step six removes the
two permissions and deletes the output.

166
00:10:54.287 --> 00:10:56.437
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

167
00:10:56.437 --> 00:10:59.940
The workbook's Chapter 3
has the exact commands.

168
00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:05.077
The federal requirement.

169
00:11:05.077 --> 00:11:09.880
Gate 2 supports NIST special
publication eight hundred fifty-three

170
00:11:09.880 --> 00:11:13.840
A C two and I A five, and the
module's table maps the evidence.

171
00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:18.015
Your organization still sets the
organization-defined parameters,

172
00:11:18.015 --> 00:11:21.165
and the assessor decides
whether each is satisfied.

173
00:11:21.165 --> 00:11:24.778
For account and authenticator
management, the proposals,

174
00:11:24.778 --> 00:11:27.940
the reviewed matrix file
and the owners' decisions.

175
00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:31.890
For privileged accounts, with
high-risk first, the findings.

176
00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:35.903
For configuration change control,
the waves and their rollback.

177
00:11:35.903 --> 00:11:39.865
For access enforcement, the
Workload I D Premium scope.

178
00:11:39.865 --> 00:11:43.340
And for continuous monitoring,
the gate's own status file.

179
00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:47.978
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers.

180
00:11:47.978 --> 00:11:52.417
The agency lines its evidence up
with what its providers report;

181
00:11:52.417 --> 00:11:54.427
it doesn't report K S I's itself.

182
00:11:54.427 --> 00:11:58.040
Which file supports which
indicator is the program's mapping.

183
00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:03.765
Check yourself with two questions.

184
00:12:03.765 --> 00:12:06.965
First: who decides an
identity's disposition?

185
00:12:06.965 --> 00:12:13.165
The answer is b: the application owner,
account by account, under decision D N P 3.

186
00:12:13.165 --> 00:12:17.853
The matrix only proposes,
and the ISSO checks the gate.

187
00:12:17.853 --> 00:12:22.503
Second: a high-risk account is
scheduled for re-platforming in wave 2.

188
00:12:22.503 --> 00:12:23.514
What happens?

189
00:12:23.514 --> 00:12:24.759
The answer is a.

190
00:12:24.759 --> 00:12:30.877
N P I M A T oh seven raises a finding,
because high-risk accounts go in wave 1.

191
00:12:30.877 --> 00:12:34.302
The code reports it; it doesn't
move the account itself.

192
00:12:34.302 --> 00:12:39.289
You saw exactly that finding
for svc hrsync in step five.

193
00:12:39.289 --> 00:12:41.717
The module page has three more questions,

194
00:12:41.717 --> 00:12:44.570
each linked to the
section that answers it.

195
00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:49.145
To sum up.

196
00:12:49.145 --> 00:12:54.328
You proposed a target for twenty identities,
checked the owners' dispositions,

197
00:12:54.328 --> 00:12:58.633
read two waves and their rollback,
and scoped Workload I D Premium.

198
00:12:58.633 --> 00:13:01.908
Gate 2 isn't met on the
fixtures, and you can say why.

199
00:13:01.908 --> 00:13:06.807
The evidence an ISSO keeps for Gate
2 from this module is the proposals,

200
00:13:06.807 --> 00:13:11.877
the decisions, the waves, the license
scope, the findings, the gate's status,

201
00:13:11.877 --> 00:13:15.932
and the manifest that hashes
them and the inputs, all dated.

202
00:13:15.932 --> 00:13:19.008
Before you ship, work through
the chapter's checklist.

203
00:13:19.008 --> 00:13:22.208
Next is Module 4: set
the credential rules.

204
00:13:22.208 --> 00:13:27.065
The tenant can block new secrets and
cap certificate lifetimes itself,

205
00:13:27.065 --> 00:13:30.820
and the rest is a rotation
plan, from renewal to removal.
